    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Amsterdam grew from a settlement at a dam on the Amstel, first named in 1275, into a trading town of the County of Holland that lived from beer, herring, and grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from the Baltic.</li><li>In the 17th century, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, it became the leading port and financial centre of the western world, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, a bank and a stock exchange, and its canals were built around the old town.</li><li>The city declined in the 18th and early 19th centuries, revived with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea-canal">North Sea Canal</a> and industry, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> lost most of its Jewish population, who had made up about a tenth of its people.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1452 a fire destroyed three quarters of Amsterdam, and Emperor Charles decreed that new houses were to be built of stone; the Wooden House in the Begijnhof is one of the few wooden buildings left from this period.</p></aside>
    </section>

